    <!-- Beyond Accuracy -->
    <section class="section" id="beyond-accuracy">
      <span class="section-label">Foundation Concepts</span>
      <h2>Why Accuracy Alone is Dangerous</h2>
      <p>Imagine you're building a fraud detection system. Your dataset contains 10,000 transactions: 9,950 legitimate (99.5%) and only 50 fraudulent (0.5%). A naive classifier that predicts every transaction as legitimate achieves 99.5% accuracy. Yet it catches zero fraudulent transactions. In this context, accuracy is a useless metric.</p>

      <p>This is the fundamental flaw of accuracy: it treats all correct predictions equally, regardless of which class they belong to. In imbalanced classification problems (fraud, disease diagnosis, churn prediction), accuracy hides poor performance on the minority class, which is often the most important class to identify correctly.</p>

      <div class="callout warning">
        <strong>Critical Rule:</strong> Never rely on accuracy alone for classification evaluation. Always examine metrics broken down by class, especially when classes are imbalanced.
      </div>

