    <!-- Precision and Recall -->
    <section class="section" id="precision-recall">
      <span class="section-label">Core Metrics</span>
      <h2>Precision and Recall: The Precision-Recall Tradeoff</h2>
      <p><strong>Precision:</strong> Of all the positive predictions your model made, how many were actually correct? High precision means few false positives. Use precision when false positives are costly (spam detection, credit card fraud where false positives upset customers).</p>

      <p><strong>Recall:</strong> Of all the actual positive cases in the data, how many did your model correctly identify? High recall means few false negatives. Use recall when false negatives are costly (disease diagnosis, where missing a sick patient is dangerous).</p>

      <p>There's always a tradeoff: you can increase recall by predicting more positives, but this typically decreases precision. The threshold you choose determines this tradeoff. Different business contexts require different thresholds.</p>

      <h3>Computing and Interpreting Metrics</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">confusion_matrix</span>, <span class="fn">precision_score</span>, <span class="fn">recall_score</span>, <span class="fn">f1_score</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Example: Cancer diagnosis classifier</span>
<span class="va">y_true</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>])
<span class="va">y_pred</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>])

<span class="cm"># Confusion matrix</span>
<span class="va">cm</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">confusion_matrix</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
<span class="cm"># [[5, 1],   -> 5 TN, 1 FP</span>
<span class="cm">#  [1, 3]]   -> 1 FN, 3 TP</span>

<span class="va">tn</span>, <span class="va">fp</span>, <span class="va">fn</span>, <span class="va">tp</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">cm</span>.<span class="fn">ravel</span>()

<span class="cm"># Calculate metrics</span>
<span class="va">precision</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tp</span> <span class="op">/</span> (<span class="va">tp</span> <span class="op">+</span> <span class="va">fp</span>)  <span class="cm"># 3 / (3 + 1) = 0.75</span>
<span class="va">recall</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tp</span> <span class="op">/</span> (<span class="va">tp</span> <span class="op">+</span> <span class="va">fn</span>)    <span class="cm"># 3 / (3 + 1) = 0.75</span>

<span class="cm"># Using scikit-learn functions</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Precision: {precision_score(y_true, y_pred):.3f}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Recall: {recall_score(y_true, y_pred):.3f}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"F1-Score: {f1_score(y_true, y_pred):.3f}"</span>)</code></pre>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <strong>When to prioritize each metric:</strong>
        <ul style="margin: 10px 0; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li><strong>High Precision:</strong> Spam detection, ad recommendation (false positives annoy users)</li>
          <li><strong>High Recall:</strong> Disease diagnosis, anomaly detection (false negatives are dangerous)</li>
          <li><strong>Balanced:</strong> Most general applications use F1-score or precision-recall curves</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- F1-Score -->
    <section class="section" id="f1-score">
      <span class="section-label">Core Metrics</span>
      <h2>F1-Score: Harmonic Mean of Precision and Recall</h2>
      <p>The F1-score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, ranging from 0 to 1 (higher is better). It's useful when you want a single metric that balances both concerns and don't know which is more important.</p>

      <p>The formula is: F1 = 2 × (precision × recall) / (precision + recall)</p>

      <p>The harmonic mean is used rather than simple averaging because it heavily penalizes extreme imbalances. If precision is 0.90 and recall is 0.10, the arithmetic mean is 0.50, but the F1-score is only 0.18, correctly reflecting that the model is actually terrible at recall.</p>

      <h3>Weighted F1 for Imbalanced Data</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">classification_report</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">datasets</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">load_breast_cancer</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">ensemble</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">RandomForestClassifier</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">model_selection</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>

<span class="cm"># Load dataset</span>
<span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">y</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">load_breast_cancer</span>(<span class="dt">return_X_y</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">True</span>)
<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">X_test</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>(<span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">y</span>, <span class="dt">test_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.2</span>)

<span class="cm"># Train model</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">RandomForestClassifier</span>()
<span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)
<span class="va">y_pred</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">predict</span>(<span class="va">X_test</span>)

<span class="cm"># Detailed classification report with F1-score</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="fn">classification_report</span>(<span class="va">y_test</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>))
<span class="cm"># Output includes precision, recall, F1-score for each class</span>
<span class="cm"># and weighted F1-score considering class imbalance</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- ROC and AUC -->
    <section class="section" id="roc-auc">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Metrics</span>
      <h2>ROC Curves and AUC: Understanding Classification Thresholds</h2>
      <p>ROC (Receiver Operating Characteristic) curves visualize the tradeoff between True Positive Rate and False Positive Rate across all possible classification thresholds. This is powerful because most classifiers don't just predict "yes" or "no"—they produce probability scores.</p>

      <p>The AUC (Area Under the Curve) is a single number (0-1) summarizing the entire ROC curve: 1.0 = perfect classifier, 0.5 = random classifier, 0.0 = inverted classifier. AUC is particularly useful for imbalanced datasets because it's threshold-independent and evaluates the model's ability to rank positive cases above negative cases.</p>

      <h3>Plotting ROC Curves</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">roc_curve</span>, <span class="fn">auc</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">linear_model</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">LogisticRegression</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">matplotlib</span>.<span class="im">pyplot</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">plt</span>

<span class="cm"># Train model that outputs probabilities</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">LogisticRegression</span>()
<span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)

<span class="cm"># Get probability predictions</span>
<span class="va">y_proba</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">predict_proba</span>(<span class="va">X_test</span>)[<span class="op">:</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>]

<span class="cm"># Calculate ROC curve</span>
<span class="va">fpr</span>, <span class="va">tpr</span>, <span class="va">thresholds</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">roc_curve</span>(<span class="va">y_test</span>, <span class="va">y_proba</span>)
<span class="va">roc_auc</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">auc</span>(<span class="va">fpr</span>, <span class="va">tpr</span>)

<span class="cm"># Plot</span>
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">plot</span>(<span class="va">fpr</span>, <span class="va">tpr</span>, <span class="dt">label</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">f'ROC (AUC = {roc_auc:.3f})'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">plot</span>([<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>], [<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>], <span class="st">'--'</span>, <span class="dt">label</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'Random'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">xlabel</span>(<span class="st">'False Positive Rate'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">ylabel</span>(<span class="st">'True Positive Rate'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">legend</span>()
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">show</span>()</code></pre>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <strong>When to use AUC:</strong> ROC-AUC is ideal for imbalanced classification and when comparing models across different thresholds. It's more informative than accuracy, precision, or recall alone for evaluating ranking ability.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Choosing Metrics -->
    <section class="section" id="choosing-metrics">
      <span class="section-label">Practical Application</span>
      <h2>Choosing the Right Metrics for Your Problem</h2>

      <h3>Email Spam Classification</h3>
      <p><strong>Business impact of errors:</strong> False positives (marking legitimate email as spam) are extremely costly—users lose important messages. False negatives (missing spam) are annoying but less critical.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended metrics:</strong> High precision, acceptable recall. Monitor precision-recall curve. Use threshold optimization to maximize precision while maintaining reasonable recall.</p>

      <h3>Credit Card Fraud Detection</h3>
      <p><strong>Business impact:</strong> False negatives (missed fraud) are very costly. False positives (flagging legitimate transactions) are acceptable—security teams can quickly investigate.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended metrics:</strong> High recall with reasonable precision. Use ROC-AUC to evaluate and optimize threshold.</p>

      <h3>Medical Diagnosis</h3>
      <p><strong>Business impact:</strong> False negatives (missing disease) can be life-threatening. False positives lead to unnecessary treatment but can be confirmed with further tests.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended metrics:</strong> Very high recall (prioritize catching all true cases). Evaluate sensitivity-specificity, use precision-recall curve to understand tradeoffs.</p>

      <h3>Product Recommendation</h3>
      <p><strong>Business impact:</strong> Both false positives (recommending wrong items) and false negatives (missing good recommendations) harm user experience equally.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended metrics:</strong> Balanced metrics. F1-score or macro-averaged precision/recall is appropriate.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Real-World Workflow -->
    <section class="section" id="workflow">
      <span class="section-label">Real-World Application</span>
      <h2>Complete Evaluation Workflow</h2>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">confusion_matrix</span>, <span class="fn">classification_report</span>, <span class="fn">roc_auc_score</span>

<span class="cm"># Complete evaluation function</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">evaluate_classifier</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>, <span class="va">y_proba</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">None</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Comprehensive classification evaluation"""</span>
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">"=== CONFUSION MATRIX ==="</span>)
    <span class="va">cm</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">confusion_matrix</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>)
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">cm</span>)

    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">"\n=== CLASSIFICATION REPORT ==="</span>)
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="fn">classification_report</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>))

    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">y_proba</span> <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="st">not</span> <span class="st">None</span>:
        <span class="va">auc</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">roc_auc_score</span>(<span class="va">y_true</span>, <span class="va">y_proba</span>)
        <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"\n=== ROC-AUC ==="</span>)
        <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"AUC: {auc:.3f}"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Usage</span>
<span class="fn">evaluate_classifier</span>(<span class="va">y_test</span>, <span class="va">y_pred</span>, <span class="va">y_proba</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

